# Comments — public to post and read, authenticated to moderate.
#
# Comments are visible the moment they are posted. There is no approval queue,
# so the defences are all at the door: a hard rate limit (RATE_LIMIT_COMMENT,
# 6/minute), length bounds on the model, a depth cap of one, and a honeypot.
#
# Two audiences, two shapes. /blogs/{slug}/comments returns PublicComment,
# which has no field for an email address and therefore cannot leak one.
# /comments returns Comment, which does — and requires a token on EVERY route,
# the list included. That is why these routes are written out rather than built
# with crud_router, whose list route is deliberately public.

# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{adminEmail}}", "password": "{{adminPassword}}" }

###

@accessToken = {{login.response.body.$.access_token}}
@slug = example-post

# ================================ public ================================

### Read the thread. Top-level comments, each with its replies, oldest first —
# a conversation reads top to bottom.
GET {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments

### Post one. `slug` comes from the path and is not accepted in the body.
# @name comment
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "author": "Anna",
  "email": "anna@example.com",
  "body": "Useful walkthrough — the bit about pinning the ref was the part I was missing."
}

###

@commentId = {{comment.response.body.$.comment.id}}

### Reply to it. Depth is capped at one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "author": "Dileepa",
  "body": "Glad it helped.",
  "parentId": "{{commentId}}"
}

### Reply to the reply -> re-parented to the same thread, not a third level.
# The reader did nothing wrong, so this is not an error.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "author": "Sam",
  "body": "Same question here.",
  "parentId": "{{commentId}}"
}

# =============================== reactions ===============================
#
# The same four as a post — liked | insightful | useful | learned — applied by
# the same service. Replies are comments, so this route works on them too and
# there is no second one.

### React to a comment.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments/{{commentId}}/reactions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reaction": "insightful" }

### Change it. The previous count goes down as the new one goes up.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments/{{commentId}}/reactions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reaction": "useful" }

### Press the one already chosen -> clears it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments/{{commentId}}/reactions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reaction": "useful" }

### Read the thread back: each comment carries its counts and whatever THIS
### caller chose, fetched in one query across the thread rather than one per
### comment.
GET {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments

### Reacting through a post the comment does not belong to -> 404. The slug in
### the path is checked against the comment; it is not decorative.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/some-other-post/comments/{{commentId}}/reactions
Content-Type: application/json

{ "reaction": "liked" }

# =============================== moderation ==============================

### Every comment, hidden ones included, newest first. 401 without the token.
GET {{baseUrl}}/comments
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Just one post's.
GET {{baseUrl}}/comments?slug={{slug}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Only the hidden ones.
GET {{baseUrl}}/comments?published=false
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Reply as the author. The ONLY path that sets `authorIsOwner` — a reader
### cannot claim the badge, because the public model has no field for it and
### extra fields are forbidden. Not rate limited: it needs a token.
POST {{baseUrl}}/comments
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "slug": "{{slug}}",
  "author": "Dileepa",
  "body": "Glad it helped — the pinned ref trips people up.",
  "parentId": "{{commentId}}"
}

### Hide one. Reversible, and the replies under it survive — they are promoted
### to top level rather than vanishing with their parent.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/comments/{{commentId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "published": false }

### Put it back.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/comments/{{commentId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "published": true }

### Delete. Permanent — prefer hiding unless the content has to go.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/comments/{{commentId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

# ============================= failure cases =============================

### The honeypot. A field no human can see, so anything that fills it is a bot.
### Returns 201 and stores nothing: telling a bot which field caught it is how
### it learns to stop tripping it. `accepted` is false.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{ "author": "Bot", "body": "Cheap watches", "honeypot": "http://spam.example" }

### Naming a different post -> 422. `CommentCreate` has no `slug` field and
### extra fields are forbidden, so this cannot be quietly ignored either.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{ "author": "Anna", "body": "Hi", "slug": "some-other-post" }

### Empty body -> 422.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/{{slug}}/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{ "author": "Anna", "body": "" }

### Commenting on an unpublished post -> 404, not 403.
POST {{baseUrl}}/blogs/example-draft/comments
Content-Type: application/json

{ "author": "Anna", "body": "Hi" }

### Listing without a token -> 401. This is the one collection an anonymous
### caller cannot list, because it holds email addresses.
GET {{baseUrl}}/comments

### Moderating without a token -> 401.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/comments/{{commentId}}
